        'INDUSTRY STYLE (native technical editor): Claim no more than the English proves: never add eşsiz, benzersiz, rakipsiz or mükemmel where the English has no such word. Keep the source\'s own units and never append a conversion it does not give: "Max Envelope: 35” x 39” x 39”" is "Maksimum çalışma alanı: 35″ x 39″ x 39″", with no "(889 x 991 mm)". One term per concept on a page: devir for rotor speed, debi for measured flow, kartuş for the turbo core. Break English chains: no "X, Y ve Z ile donatılmış olup" runs, no 3-deep possessive stacks, no filler "olarak".',
        'A MACHINE OR SYSTEM NAME IS REBUILT, NOT TRANSLATED WORD BY WORD: front every English qualifier that trails the head noun or hangs off "with", and never let it land on the neighbouring noun. "Vertical balancing machine standard" is "Standart dikey balans makinesi"; "Engine cooling fan with motor balancing machine" is "Motorlu motor soğutma fanı balans makinesi"; "Fan and motor balancing machine" is "Fan ve motor balans makinesi", never "Fan ve motorlu balans makinesi". Inside a NAME the head is "balans makinesi", never the process noun "balanslama makinesi". A card lead "<Brand> <Product> system for <use> applications." becomes "<Brand> <Product> sistemi, <use> için tasarlanmıştır." If the Turkish shows three -ı/-i/-u/-ü possessive heads in a row, rebuild it; render "a wide range of X" as "çok çeşitli X", never "geniş bir X yelpazesi"; and never gloss a word you already translated ("kartuş (core)" is a defect). A product name that trails its generic noun in English moves in front of it: "Newest measuring and control unit ROTORTEST" is "En yeni ROTORTEST ölçüm ve kontrol ünitesi".',
        'AN ENGLISH NOUN USED AS A MODIFIER TAKES -lI/-lU, NOT THE BARE STEM: "roll brake test system" is "rulolu fren test sistemi"; "half case RDU/PTU" is "yarım gövdeli RDU/PTU"; "residential appliances" is "konut tipi cihazlar". Parts and features take -lI on EVERY coordinated noun, never "...-e sahip": "Pedestals with integrated pins and V-blocks" is "Entegre pimli ve V-bloklu yatak ayakları", never "Entegre pimler ve V-blokları olan"; "su soğutmalı", "servo tahrikli"; keep "...olan" for one owned part ("kendi muylusu olan rotorlar"). Never use "sahip olmak" for credentials or abstractions: degrees are "almak" or "mezun olmak", opportunities "elde etmek", capabilities "kazanmak". A span takes the ablative-dative pair: "tek kanallıdan 60 kanallıya kadar". Never reproduce an English hyphen chain word for word: "<X>-as-a-service" inverts to "hizmet olarak balanslama (BaaS)"; "end-of-line" is "hat sonu" with no genitive. Coordinate two nouns with "ve", not "ile" ("gürültü ve titreşim"); keep "ile" for a genuine instrument or the outer members of a nested list.',
        'A SPEC OR FEATURE BULLET IS A NOUN PHRASE, AND AN ADJECTIVE STAYS AN ADJECTIVE: "Check relief pressure" in a check list is "Tahliye basıncı kontrolü", never "...kontrol edin"; "Easily add new models" is "Yeni modellerin kolayca eklenmesi"; "Compatible with most common leak detectors" ends in "...dedektörleriyle uyumlu". A description of what we deliver never becomes an imperative: "Periodic verification of machine operations" is "Makine çalışmasının periyodik doğrulaması". "Interfaces with X" is "X ile entegre çalışır". A compliance bullet puts the standard first and ends in "uygun": "Safety norms ISO 21940-23:2012 compliant" is "ISO 21940-23:2012 güvenlik normlarına uygun". In a "<measure> with <configuration>" row the configuration is a condition, never a second noun: "Maximum distance between bearers with standard bed" is "Standart yatakta yatak mesnetleri arasındaki maksimum mesafe", never "Standart yatakla ... arasındaki" (= between the bed and the bearers); "with/without belt drive" is "kayış tahrikiyle/kayış tahriki olmadan". Keep one shape across a sibling list.',
        'PRESS HEADLINES ARE SENTENCE CASE WITH A SUBJECT COMMA, BUT THE JOB TITLE INSIDE IS CAPITALISED WORD BY WORD, its division or scope included, scope before rank, no comma: "Hires Jack Pennuto as Executive Vice President, Aerospace & Industrials" is "Jack Pennuto\'yu Havacılık-Uzay ve Sanayi İcra Başkan Yardımcısı olarak işe aldı". A functional scope takes "-dAn Sorumlu": "Senior Vice President of Global Manufacturing Operations" is "Küresel Üretim Operasyonlarından Sorumlu Kıdemli Başkan Yardımcısı". A noun modifier in a title is singular: "Director of Operations" is "Operasyon Direktörü". The business unit Aerospace & Industrials is a proper name, "Havacılık-Uzay ve Sanayi", capitalised the same in titles, headlines, tabs and chips. Seniority is exact: Senior VP = "Kıdemli Başkan Yardımcısı", VP = "Başkan Yardımcısı", Executive VP = "İcra Başkan Yardımcısı", CTO = "Teknoloji Direktörü (CTO)". Press verbs keep the fact: Hires = "işe aldı", Appoints/Names = "atadı", Welcomes = "ekibine kattı", Joins = "katıldı", Promotes = "...-e terfi ettirdi"; "Announces Erin Dillard as Executive Vice President of Ascential Care" is "Erin Dillard\'ın Ascential Care İcra Başkan Yardımcılığına getirildiğini duyurdu", never "Erin Dillard\'ı ... olarak duyurdu". Keep the dateline as the source writes it ("Shelby Township, Mich.,") with a long date ("25 Ağustos 2026").',
        'NUMBER, DATE AND UNIT CONVENTIONS ARE ONE PER DOCUMENT: the decimal separator is a comma in every figure, formula and tolerance ("1,5 milyon", "0,0065 mm", "9,81"); the thousands separator is a period from five digits up ("13.000") while a four-digit technical figure may stay unseparated ("2500 d/d"); currency follows the number ("1.500 TL"); time is 24-hour; a form date is DD.MM.YYYY but a press date is long ("25 Ağustos 2026"). Per-minute units are Turkish - l/dk, kg/dk - and rotational speed is "d/d", never "rpm". A space separates every number from its unit symbol ("50-60 Hz", "400 V", "16 A", "350 bar"; only "%100" is closed up), an exponent is a real superscript ("10⁻²"), a span takes a plain hyphen ("6-10 kat"). Never put -lAr on a noun after a number or in a stat counter: "13.000 proje", "40 lokasyon".',
        'COMPANY BOILERPLATE IS REWRITTEN IN TURKISH BUSINESS IDIOM, THEN REUSED CHARACTER FOR CHARACTER ON EVERY PAGE THAT CARRIES IT: "global footprint" is "küresel faaliyet ağı"; "the expertise of 2,300 professionals across 40 locations" is "40 lokasyondaki 2.300 profesyonelin bilgi birikimiyle"; "the company\'s uniqueness lies in" is "Şirketi benzersiz kılan"; "Backed by the brands you trust. Powered by the quality you expect." is "Güvendiğiniz markaların desteğiyle. Beklediğiniz kalitenin gücüyle." An English passive slogan becomes active Turkish: "We\'re driven by purpose and powered by expertise." is "Bizi amacımız yönlendirir, gücümüzü uzmanlığımızdan alırız." Take the Turkish pairing over the dictionary word: sharp minds = "parlak zihinler", deep expertise = "engin uzmanlık", make ideas real = "hayata geçirmek", "Stay connected" = "Bizi takip edin". What we do to a machine: designed or engineered = "tasarlanmıştır" (never the calque "mühendislik edilmiştir"), built or manufactured = "üretilmiştir"; "geliştirilmiştir" only where the source says developed; never "inşa etmek" for a machine. A machine fault is "arıza", a human assembly mistake "hata".',
        'A TITLE TAIL, A CHIP LIST AND QUOTED SPEECH EACH HAVE ONE FIXED SHAPE. In "<Descriptor> - Ascential Test & Measurement" translate only the descriptor, in sentence case, and leave the brand tail in English: "Axle Balancers - Ascential Test & Measurement" is "Aks balans makineleri - Ascential Test & Measurement", never "Axle Balancers - Ascential Test ve Ölçüm". In a comma-separated taxonomy chip list every chip matches its enforced term exactly: "Balancing, Refrigeration & Gas Management, Aerospace & Industrials" is "Balanslama, Soğutma ve gaz yönetimi, Havacılık-Uzay ve Sanayi". Quoted speech opens with “ and closes with ” (“Alessandro, proje yönetiminde...”); a suffix on a proper noun takes one straight ASCII apostrophe ("Ascential\'in"). "&" between two words is "ve" in every label, including a fragment that ends in it: "Service &amp;" is "Servis ve". In a "<Category> - <Subtype>" label both halves start with a capital: "Yatay manuel - Su sayaçları", "Ataşmanlar - Hat sonu".',
        'CONSISTENCY, THE LARGEST DEFECT CLASS: one English term or label gets exactly ONE Turkish rendering everywhere it appears - nav, card, spec table, FAQ, body, footer, <title> and meta - and a row label repeated in a second table repeats the first table\'s wording ("Review" is "Değerlendirme" in both). Group the strings that share an English term and force them to agree: "propulsion" is never both "tahrik" and "itki". Two synonyms for one on-screen object is a defect even when both are correct Turkish.',
        'FIDELITY: never add, drop, strengthen or weaken a claim, modifier, number, qualifier or hedge. "Addresses manufacturing process deficiencies" is "üretim süreci eksikliklerini ele alır", never "ortadan kaldırır"; "will confirm shortly" is "en kısa sürede", never "birkaç dakika içinde"; "Turbocharger flow benches" is "turboşarj akış tezgâhları" and never loses "Turbocharger"; "World\'s leading measuring system" keeps the superlative. Hedged offers stay hedged: "help solve X" is "...çözmenize nasıl yardımcı olabileceğimizi", never "...nasıl çözeceğimizi". Repeat the superlative marker "en" on every coordinated adjective: "en zorlu ve en karmaşık".',
        'A BRAND, PRODUCT, DIVISION OR MODEL NAME IS NEVER TRANSLATED, DECLINED AWAY OR HALF-TRANSLATED, in any surface: XL Machine, ADAS Garage, Cimat, Galileo, Kleinknecht, Lismar, Titan, Burke Porter, Universal Balancing, WinterPark Engineering, Bauer, Sentia, Ascential Care, Ascentialytics, Ascential Medical & Life Sciences, and model codes such as CMT-TR, CMT-VSR, LGC30XX, UNI-64. A legal name keeps every word ("Engineering" too); translate only the generic head noun around it ("Ascential Medical & Life Sciences bölümü"). Model codes and acronyms keep their capitals: "HC DS02", not "hc ds02"; "CNC", not "cnc". A trademarked English phrase keeps its TM on the English, never on a Turkish paraphrase.',
        'TRANSLATE A HEADING SPLIT ACROSS TWO LINES, SPANS OR SEGMENTS AS ONE TURKISH PHRASE, resolving the compound term first and putting the single Turkish word in the accented span: "VERTICAL / balancing machines" is "DİKEY balans makineleri"; "EDDY / current technology" is "girdap akımı teknolojisi", never reading "current" as present-day. The split fragments and the joined heading must yield identical Turkish.',
        'TURKISH NOUN COMPOUNDS TAKE THE POSSESSIVE ON THE HEAD NOUN (izafet): "landing gear test" is "iniş takımı testi", "metals automation" is "metal otomasyonu", "Paper & Plastic Industry" is "Kâğıt ve plastik sanayii", "HVAC blowers" is "HVAC üfleyicileri". A label ending in a bare "test", "otomasyon", "montaj" or "sanayi" after a modifying noun is ungrammatical; where compounds would stack, use "için": "Automotive Industry Balancers" is "Otomotiv sanayii için balans makineleri". Do not strand a possessive on a coordinated item that is not the head: "yedek parça ve analitik", not "...analitiği".',
        'CHECK THE CASE THE MAIN VERB LICENSES BEFORE FINISHING THE CLAUSE, AND PUT IT ON EVERY COORDINATED OBJECT: etkilemek, artırmak, azaltmak, iyileştirmek all take the accusative, so "süresini, sağlığını, onarımları ve daha pek çok alanı etkileyebilir" - no item may drift into the locative ("alanda"), which leaves a transitive verb with no object.',
