        'IN "help someone do X" THE POSSESSIVE ON THE VERBAL NOUN GOES ON THE CUSTOMER AND THE VERBAL NOUN TAKES THE DATIVE: "hangi zorlukları çözmenize yardımcı olabiliriz?", never "çözmede size yardımcı olabiliriz" and never "-memize", which reverses who acts.',
        'PRESERVE THE SUBJECT AND THE AGENT OF THE ENGLISH SENTENCE, AND BACK-TRANSLATE ANY TWO-PARTICIPANT CLAUSE BEFORE MOVING ON: "understand how our site is used" is "sitemizin nasıl kullanıldığını anlamak", not "sitenizi nasıl kullandığımızı". "Water-cooled AC motors" is "su soğutmalı AC motorlar", never motors that cool water. Never insert a comitative -le/-la that turns the company\'s own brands into third-party partners.',
        'CORRELATIVES (ister...ister, hem...hem, ne...ne, gerek...gerek) MUST CARRY THE PARTICLE ON EVERY BRANCH, WITH EVERY BRANCH IN THE SAME SHAPE AND THE SHARED VERB AFTER THE LAST ONE: "İster sıfırdan başlayalım ister mevcut teknolojilerden yola çıkalım, ...". Never pair a verb-bearing branch with a bare noun or postpositional phrase, and never mix "ister" with "veya" or "ya da".',
        'NEVER OPEN A TURKISH SENTENCE WITH A COORDINATING CONJUNCTION - not "Ve", "Veya", "Ya da", "Ama" or "Fakat". English sentence-initial "And..." / "But..." becomes "Ayrıca...", "Bunun yanı sıra...", "Ancak..." placed inside the clause, or the branch folds back into the previous sentence as a coordinate noun phrase after a semicolon. Both branches of such a pair share one grammatical shape: two noun phrases or two finite clauses, never one of each. Do not use "veya" twice at two syntactic levels in one clause: use "ya da" for the outer alternative and keep "veya" for the inner list.',
