        'NEVER LEAVE AN ENGLISH WORD IN A DESCRIPTOR, ATTRIBUTE OR WIDGET: aria-label, title, alt, placeholder, consent and chat chrome are fully Turkish ("Ascential Technologies\'i takip edin", not "Follow Ascential Technologies"). Translate a descriptor around its acronym or brand-like words: "NDT inspection" is "tahribatsız muayene (NDT)", "First alert monitoring system" is "İlk uyarı izleme sistemi", "SME" (subject matter expert) is "konu uzmanı". An English common noun never takes a Turkish suffix: "Field Balancers" is "Saha balans cihazları", never "Field Balancer\'lar". Trade loanwords a Turkish engineer says out loud stay (HVAC, OBD, NVH, ADAS, dinamometre), but never beside a translated sibling: "HVAC fan & blower balancing" is "HVAC fan ve üfleyici balanslama".',
        'NEVER LEAVE A BARE ADJECTIVE AS A STANDALONE LINK, TAB OR LIST LABEL, and never strand an English adjective before a noun or question word: "Medical" as an affiliate link is "Medikal", never "Tıbbi"; "Industrial" as a market or division label is "Sanayi", but before a machine or unit it is "endüstriyel" ("Industrial balancing machine" is "Endüstriyel balans makinesi", never "Sanayi tipi balans makinesi"); "Aerospace or Industrial challenges" is "havacılık veya sanayi alanındaki zorluklar". Coordinated modifiers inside one phrase share a part of speech: "tıp ve yaşam bilimleri ile sanayi pazarlarında".',
        'USE THE SHOP-FLOOR SENSE OF ANY WORD THAT HAS AN EVERYDAY HOMONYM: field service = "saha" (not "alan"); machine down = "arızalı"; "column" = "kolon"; throughput = "üretim hızı"; the efficiency of a machine, motor or pump = "verim" ("mekanik verim", "yüksek güç altında verim"), "verimlilik" only for productivity; sensitive to disturbance = "duyarlı", while "hassas" means precise; an accurate machine "yüksek doğrulukla çalışır", never "doğrudur"; embedded = "entegre" ("gömülü" for a part set into the machine body), never "hat içi" (= in-line); a machine guard = "muhafaza", not "koruma"; drive a pump or shaft = "tahrik etmek" (not "sürmek"); a roller on a paper, conveyor or mill line = "merdane", on a brake or chassis tester = "rulo"; a field or service agent is one person, "temsilci", never "ekip"; cost of failure = "hatanın bedeli"; inquiry or support case = "talep"; asset on a service form = "ekipman"; an offering = "hizmet" or "çözüm" (not "sunum"); a content hub or a bare "Hub" nav label = "Merkez", a wheel hub = "poyra"; a Location form field = "Konum".',
        'STATUTORY AND LEGAL PAGE NAMES HAVE FIXED TURKISH EQUIVALENTS, NOT LITERAL ONES, AND A DEFINED TERM IS A FROZEN STRING: Imprint/Impressum = "Künye" (never "Yasal uyarı"); Terms = "Kullanım koşulları" (never bare "Koşullar"); Privacy Policy = "Gizlilik politikası"; Cookie Policy = "Çerez politikası". Declare a defined term once and repeat it character for character; "Name:" is "Ad Soyad:" and "Date:" is "Tarih:". Never drop or renumber a clause number.',
        'NEVER CARRY AN ENGLISH SLASH-PLURAL OR SLASH-ALTERNATIVE INTO TURKISH: "issue/s" is "sorunlar", "part/s" is "parçalar", "day/s" is "günler". Turkish has no "/lar" convention.',
        'AN ADVERB OR ADJECTIVE THAT DESCRIBES HOW WE WORK ATTACHES TO THE VERB, NOT TO AN INANIMATE NOUN: "allowing us to adapt swiftly" is "hızla uyum sağlamamıza olanak tanır"; "we engineer uncompromising possibilities" is "ödün vermeden ... olanaklar geliştiririz", never "ödün vermeyen olanaklar". Adverbial phrases of circumstance stay bare next to the verb: "baskı altında mevcut bir çözümü optimize etmek", never "baskı altındaki bir çözüm", which relocates the pressure onto the equipment.',
