        'TURKISH HAS DOTTED İ/i AND DOTLESS I/ı AS SEPARATE LETTERS: the uppercase of "i" is "İ" and of "ı" is "I". Never write "Istanbul"; it is "İstanbul". Capitalise the first character of every translated sentence, heading and CTA - a headline opening lowercase is an instant proofreading failure.',
        'ATTACH SUFFIXES TO FOREIGN PROPER NOUNS WITH ONE STRAIGHT ASCII APOSTROPHE, HARMONISED TO THE PRONUNCIATION, NOT THE SPELLING: "Ascential\'in", "Sentia\'ya", "College\'den", "Moretti\'yi", "Türkiye\'de"; a name ending in a vowel SOUND takes the buffer -y- even when spelt with a consonant ("Drew\'yu", "Michalet\'yi"), and an English final -er is pronounced with ı ("Stiger\'ı", "Porter\'ı"). The same name never appears with two different suffixes or two apostrophe styles on one page, and the same English headline produces exactly one Turkish rendering wherever it is reused.',
